<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this note we consider three results of Morita: <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, (1.3)]</cite>,
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, (1.7)]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, (5.1)]</cite>, which give relations
between certain two dimensional cohomology classes of various moduli spaces of
curves. We have reformulated Morita’s results in more geometric language to
facilitate their application in our work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> on the Arakelov
geometry of <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and to advertise them to algebraic geometers. We give
a new, and hopefully more geometric, proof of each result. In the last
section, we give a geometric interpretation of another of Morita’s results
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, (5.4)]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Denote the mapping class group of a closed orientable surface of genus
<math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and the mapping class group of a pointed, closed orientable
surface by <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Denote the moduli space of smooth projective curves over <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>
(i.e., compact Riemann surfaces of genus <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>)
by <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and the universal curve over it by <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote the
moduli space of principally polarized abelian varieties of dimension <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>
by <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. As is customary, we view each of these as an orbifold, or,
more accurately, a stack in the sense of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. The orbifold
fundamental groups of <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphic to</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{g},\,{\Gamma}_{g}^{1}\text{ and }Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{g},\,{\Gamma}_{g}^{1}\text{ and }Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">respectively, (except when <math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> when
<math id="S1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}({\mathcal{C}}_{1},*)\cong SL_{2}({\mathbb{Z}})\ltimes{\mathbb{Z}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋉</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}({\mathcal{C}}_{1},*)\cong SL_{2}({\mathbb{Z}})\ltimes{\mathbb{Z}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{1}={\Gamma}_{1}^{1}\cong SL_{2}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{1}={\Gamma}_{1}^{1}\cong SL_{2}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>);
the isomorphisms are unique up to inner automorphisms. Each of these
moduli spaces is a <math id="S1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\pi,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\pi,1)</annotation></semantics></math> in the orbifold sense, so that for each
of these moduli spaces <math id="S1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(X,{\mathbb{Q}})\cong H^{\bullet}(\pi_{1}(X),{\mathbb{Q}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(X,{\mathbb{Q}})\cong H^{\bullet}(\pi_{1}(X),{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes its orbifold fundamental group.
The period map <math id="S1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> takes the moduli point <math id="S1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[C]</annotation></semantics></math> of the
<math id="S1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> to that of its jacobian <math id="S1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\operatorname{Jac}C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Jac</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\operatorname{Jac}C]</annotation></semantics></math>. It corresponds to the canonical
homomorphism <math id="S1.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{g}\to Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{g}\to Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive integer. The moduli space of smooth
projective curves of genus <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> with a level <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> structure will be denoted
by <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}[l]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}[l]</annotation></semantics></math> and the universal curve over it by <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}[l]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}[l]</annotation></semantics></math>. Definitions of
these moduli spaces and the corresponding mapping class groups, <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{g}[l]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{g}[l]</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{g}^{1}[l]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{g}^{1}[l]</annotation></semantics></math>, can be found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, §3,§7.4]</cite>.
Note that for all <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})=H^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g}[l],{\mathbb{Q}})^{Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}}/l{\mathbb{Z}})}\text{ and }H^{\bullet}({\mathcal{C}}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})=H^{\bullet}({\mathcal{C}}_{g}[l],{\mathbb{Q}})^{Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}}/l{\mathbb{Z}})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})=H^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g}[l],{\mathbb{Q}})^{Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}}/l{\mathbb{Z}})}\text{ and }H^{\bullet}({\mathcal{C}}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})=H^{\bullet}({\mathcal{C}}_{g}[l],{\mathbb{Q}})^{Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}}/l{\mathbb{Z}})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">In particular, the cohomology of moduli space with no level is always a
subspace of the cohomology of the moduli space with a level structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hodge bundle</span> is the rank <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> vector bundle over <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> whose
fiber over the moduli point <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[A]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[A]</annotation></semantics></math> of the abelian variety <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1,0}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1,0}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>. Its first Chern class is denoted <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, as is its
pullback to <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> along the period map. It is known that for each
<math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> generates <math id="S1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\mathcal{A}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\mathcal{A}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. (In fact,
in both cases, the determinant <math id="S1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the Hodge bundle generates the
Picard group of the corresponding moduli functor, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> be the first Chern class of the relative <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cotangent</span> bundle <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{{\omega}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{{\omega}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
of the projection <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is well known that <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>
has basis <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math>. The class <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> is often denoted by <math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>
in the physics literature.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
          Recall that <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{1}=12\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{1}=12\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
Morita denotes <math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="footnote1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="footnote1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\psi</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="footnote1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">One has the universal abelian variety <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. This has (orbifold)
fundamental group isomorphic to <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp(H_{\mathbb{Z}})\ltimes H_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋉</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp(H_{\mathbb{Z}})\ltimes H_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>
denotes the first homology group of the reference abelian variety. The
splitting is given by the zero section. The pullback of <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}</annotation></semantics></math> along
the period map is the jacobian of the universal curve.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Since the universal abelian
variety is a flat bundle of real tori, the projection is a foliated
bundle. It follows that there is a closed 2-form on <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}</annotation></semantics></math> whose restriction
to each fiber is the invariant form that corresponds to the polarization
(i.e., the invariant bilinear form on <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>), that is parallel with
respect to the flat structure, and whose restriction to the zero section
is trivial (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, §1]</cite>). We shall denote the cohomology
class of this form by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in H^{2}({\mathcal{J}},{\mathbb{Q}})\cong H^{2}(Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\ltimes H_{\mathbb{Z}},{\mathbb{Q}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋉</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in H^{2}({\mathcal{J}},{\mathbb{Q}})\cong H^{2}(Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\ltimes H_{\mathbb{Z}},{\mathbb{Q}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">The class <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is not integral, but <math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, (1.2)]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Denote the canonical divisor of the Riemann surface <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define a map <math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{J}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{J}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the universal curve to
the universal abelian variety by taking the point <math id="S1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[C,x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[C,x]</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
to</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g-2)x-K_{C}\in\operatorname{Jac}C" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Jac</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g-2)x-K_{C}\in\operatorname{Jac}C</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Morita <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, (1.7)]</cite>)<span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa^{\ast}\phi=2g(g-1){\omega}-6\lambda\in H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa^{\ast}\phi=2g(g-1){\omega}-6\lambda\in H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">A theta characteristic of a compact Riemann surface <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a square
root of its canonical bundle <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>; that is, a divisor class <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> such
that <math id="S1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\alpha=K_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\alpha=K_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each theta characteristic <math id="S1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> determines a
divisor <math id="S1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> in the jacobian of <math id="S1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
If one passes to the level 2 moduli space <math id="S1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}[2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}[2]</annotation></semantics></math>,
one can consistently choose a theta characteristic for each curve.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
          Actually, one has such sections defined over the moduli space
of pairs <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C,\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C,\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="footnote2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth projective curve of genus <math id="footnote2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="footnote2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is a theta characteristic. This moduli space has two components;
one for the even theta characteristics, the other for the odd ones. Since
theta characteristics correspond the <math id="footnote2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}/2</annotation></semantics></math> quadratic forms on <math id="footnote2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}(C,{\mathbb{Z}}/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}(C,{\mathbb{Z}}/2)</annotation></semantics></math>
associated to the intersection pairing, all theta characteristics will be
defined over <math id="footnote2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}[2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}[2]</annotation></semantics></math>, the moduli space of compact Riemann surfaces
with a full level 2 structure. We prefer to work with this space which
dominates the two moduli spaces of curves with theta characteristic.
It is known by the results of Harer
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and Foisy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> that the second cohomology
with <math id="footnote2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> coefficients of <math id="footnote2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}[2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}[2]</annotation></semantics></math> and the spin moduli spaces of
pointed curves are isomorphic to <math id="footnote2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, but we do not
need such deep results in this paper.</span></span></span> In this case we can define a
map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{\alpha}:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}[2]\to{\mathcal{J}}[2]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{\alpha}:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}[2]\to{\mathcal{J}}[2]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p10.2" class="ltx_p">by taking <math id="S1.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[C,x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[C,x]</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g-1)x-\alpha\in\operatorname{Jac}C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Jac</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g-1)x-\alpha\in\operatorname{Jac}C</annotation></semantics></math>. Observe that
<math id="S1.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=2j_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=2j_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">For each such <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a theta divisor <math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>
in <math id="S1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}[2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}[2]</annotation></semantics></math>, the universal abelian variety over <math id="S1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}[2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}[2]</annotation></semantics></math>, the moduli
space of principally polarized abelian varieties with a level 2
structure. Any two such
theta divisors differ by a point of order 2 of <math id="S1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}[2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}[2]</annotation></semantics></math>. The rational
homology class of <math id="S1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is therefore independent of <math id="S1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>
and is the pullback of a class in <math id="S1.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\mathcal{J}}[2],{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\mathcal{J}}[2],{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> that we denote by
<math id="S1.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">The following result is presumably well known; a proof is given in
Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Proof of Theorem ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi=\theta-\lambda/2\in H^{2}({\mathcal{J}}[2],{\mathbb{Q}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi=\theta-\lambda/2\in H^{2}({\mathcal{J}}[2],{\mathbb{Q}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> both lie in the subspace <math id="S1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\mathcal{J}},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\mathcal{J}},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows
that <math id="S1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in H^{2}({\mathcal{J}},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in H^{2}({\mathcal{J}},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">We shall prove the following result which is easily seen, using the
proposition, to be equivalent to Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Morita [, (1.7)]). ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{\alpha}^{\ast}\theta=\binom{g}{2}{\omega}-\lambda\in H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g}[2],{\mathbb{Q}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{\alpha}^{\ast}\theta=\binom{g}{2}{\omega}-\lambda\in H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g}[2],{\mathbb{Q}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Denote the square</p>
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\times_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\times_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p15.2" class="ltx_p">of the universal curve
by <math id="S1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}^{2}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}^{2}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is the moduli space of triples <math id="S1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C,x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C,x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S1.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> are arbitrary points of <math id="S1.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, a compact Riemann surface. There is a
canonical projection <math id="S1.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}^{2}_{g}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}^{2}_{g}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then have a commutative
square</p>
<table id="S1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{CD}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2}@&gt;{{\delta}}&gt;{}&gt;{\mathcal{J}}\cr @V{}V{}V@V{}V{}V\cr{\mathcal{M}}_{g}@&gt;{}&gt;{}&gt;{\mathcal{A}}_{g}\cr\end{CD}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mover><mover class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy"><mo class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy">→</mo><mspace style="width:30.0pt;" width="30.0pt"></mspace></mover><mi>δ</mi></mover></mtd><mtd><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo mathsize="1.600em" stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="1.600em" stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><mover class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy"><mo class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy">→</mo><mspace style="width:30.0pt;" width="30.0pt"></mspace></mover></mtd><mtd><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{CD}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2}@&gt;{{\delta}}&gt;{}&gt;{\mathcal{J}}\cr @V{}V{}V@V{}V{}V\cr{\mathcal{M}}_{g}@&gt;{}&gt;{}&gt;{\mathcal{A}}_{g}\cr\end{CD}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p15.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> is the difference map which is defined by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\delta}:[C,x,y]\mapsto[x]-[y]\in\operatorname{Jac}C." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Jac</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\delta}:[C,x,y]\mapsto[x]-[y]\in\operatorname{Jac}C.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">The diagonal copy of <math id="S1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a divisor and thus has
a class <math id="S1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S1.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>, denote the first Chern
class of the relative cotangent bundle of the <math id="S1.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th projection
<math id="S1.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S1.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. (That is, <math id="S1.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}=p_{j}^{\ast}({\omega})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}=p_{j}^{\ast}({\omega})</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Morita <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, (1.3)]</cite>)<span id="Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{*}(\phi)={\Delta}+(\psi_{1}+\psi_{2})/2\in H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2},{\mathbb{Q}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{*}(\phi)={\Delta}+(\psi_{1}+\psi_{2})/2\in H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2},{\mathbb{Q}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Combining this with Proposition <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> we obtain:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{\ast}(\theta)={\Delta}+(\lambda+\psi_{1}+\psi_{2})/2\in H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2}[2],{\mathbb{Q}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{\ast}(\theta)={\Delta}+(\lambda+\psi_{1}+\psi_{2})/2\in H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2}[2],{\mathbb{Q}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p18.1" class="ltx_p">In order to state the third theorem, we need a generalization of the
construction of <math id="S1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the universal abelian variety. Suppose that <math id="S1.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is an <math id="S1.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> module and that the <math id="S1.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> action on
<math id="S1.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\mathbb{R}}:=W_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\mathbb{R}}:=W_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> extends to an action of the Lie group <math id="S1.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp_{g}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp_{g}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
One can then form the corresponding flat (orbifold) bundle <math id="S1.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> of
tori over <math id="S1.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. There are three ways to view this:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S1.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\ltimes W_{\mathbb{Z}})\backslash(Sp_{g}({\mathbb{R}})\ltimes W_{\mathbb{R}})/U(g)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋉</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋉</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\ltimes W_{\mathbb{Z}})\backslash(Sp_{g}({\mathbb{R}})\ltimes W_{\mathbb{R}})/U(g)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which maps to <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}=Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash Sp_{g}({\mathbb{R}})/U(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}=Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash Sp_{g}({\mathbb{R}})/U(g)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> is the quotient of the flat bundle over <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> associated
to <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> by the local system over <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> underlies a variation of Hodge structure of odd weight
over <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> can be identified with the corresponding family
of Griffiths intermediate jacobians over <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p19.1" class="ltx_p">There are three cases of interest to us:</p>
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\mathbb{Z}}=H_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\mathbb{Z}}=H_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}(W)={\mathcal{J}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}(W)={\mathcal{J}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the universal abelian variety.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\mathbb{Z}}=\Lambda^{3}H_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\mathbb{Z}}=\Lambda^{3}H_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>; this is a variation of Hodge structure and
<math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}(\Lambda^{3}H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}(\Lambda^{3}H)</annotation></semantics></math> is the bundle of intermediate jacobians associated with
the local system over <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> with fiber <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{3}(A,{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{3}(A,{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[A]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[A]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\mathbb{Z}}=\Lambda^{3}H_{\mathbb{Z}}/H_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\mathbb{Z}}=\Lambda^{3}H_{\mathbb{Z}}/H_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is imbedded in <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{3}H_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{3}H_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> by wedging with the polarization <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta\in\Lambda^{2}H_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta\in\Lambda^{2}H_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is a variation of Hodge structure and <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}(\Lambda^{3}H/H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}(\Lambda^{3}H/H)</annotation></semantics></math> is the
bundle of intermediate jacobians associated with the primitive part of
the local system over <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> whose fiber over <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[A]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[A]</annotation></semantics></math> is the ‘primitive
part’ of <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{3}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{3}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p20.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose now that <math id="S1.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> has an <math id="S1.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant skew symmetric bilinear
form <math id="S1.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> (as each of the examples above does.) This corresponds to an
<math id="S1.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> invariant cohomology class <math id="S1.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in H^{2}(W_{\mathbb{R}}/W_{\mathbb{Z}},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in H^{2}(W_{\mathbb{R}}/W_{\mathbb{Z}},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The projection <math id="S1.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}(W)\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}(W)\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a foliated bundle of tori. Consequently, there is a closed 2-form on
<math id="S1.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> whose restriction to each fiber is the invariant form that
represents the class of <math id="S1.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, that is parallel with respect to the flat
structure, and whose restriction to the zero section is trivial (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, §1]</cite>). We shall denote the cohomology class of this
form in <math id="S1.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\mathcal{J}}(W),{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\mathcal{J}}(W),{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S1.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(W)</annotation></semantics></math>. The class <math id="S1.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\phi(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\phi(W)</annotation></semantics></math> is always
integral.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p21.1" class="ltx_p">There are lifts
<math id="S1.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu:{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{J}}(\Lambda^{3}H/H)\text{ and }\mu:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{J}}(\Lambda^{3}H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu:{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{J}}(\Lambda^{3}H/H)\text{ and }\mu:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{J}}(\Lambda^{3}H)</annotation></semantics></math>
of the period maps</p>
<table id="S1.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}\text{ and }{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}\text{ and }{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p21.2" class="ltx_p">such that the diagram</p>
<table id="S1.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{CD}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}@&gt;{\mu}&gt;{}&gt;{\mathcal{J}}(\Lambda^{3}H)\cr @V{}V{}V@V{}V{}V\cr{\mathcal{M}}_{g}@&gt;{\nu}&gt;{}&gt;{\mathcal{J}}(\Lambda^{3}H/H)\cr\end{CD}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><mover><mover class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy"><mo class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy">→</mo><mspace style="width:30.0pt;" width="30.0pt"></mspace></mover><mi>μ</mi></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo mathsize="1.600em" stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="1.600em" stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><mover><mover class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy"><mo class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy">→</mo><mspace style="width:30.0pt;" width="30.0pt"></mspace></mover><mi>ν</mi></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{CD}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}@&gt;{\mu}&gt;{}&gt;{\mathcal{J}}(\Lambda^{3}H)\cr @V{}V{}V@V{}V{}V\cr{\mathcal{M}}_{g}@&gt;{\nu}&gt;{}&gt;{\mathcal{J}}(\Lambda^{3}H/H)\cr\end{CD}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p21.3" class="ltx_p">commutes and the composite of <math id="S1.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> with the map
<math id="S1.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}(\Lambda^{3}H)\to{\mathcal{J}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}(\Lambda^{3}H)\to{\mathcal{J}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> induced by the contraction <math id="S1.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c:\Lambda^{3}H\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c:\Lambda^{3}H\to H</annotation></semantics></math>
defined in Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Some Linear Algebra ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> is the map <math id="S1.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{J}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{J}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> of
Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Morita [, (1.7)]). ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
The map <math id="S1.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is the normal function of the algebraic cycle in the universal
jacobian <math id="S1.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> whose fiber over the point <math id="S1.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[C,x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[C,x]</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is the
algebraic cycle <math id="S1.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{x}-C_{x}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{x}-C_{x}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p21.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Jac}C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Jac</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Jac}C</annotation></semantics></math>. More details can be found in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, §6]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.
The period maps <math id="S1.p21.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p21.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> induce homomorphisms</p>
<table id="S1.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{g}\to Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\ltimes\left(\Lambda^{3}H_{\mathbb{Z}}/H_{\mathbb{Z}}\right)\text{ and }{\Gamma}_{g}^{1}\to Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\ltimes\Lambda^{3}H_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋉</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋉</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{g}\to Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\ltimes\left(\Lambda^{3}H_{\mathbb{Z}}/H_{\mathbb{Z}}\right)\text{ and }{\Gamma}_{g}^{1}\to Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\ltimes\Lambda^{3}H_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p21.4" class="ltx_p">on fundamental groups. In both cases, the restriction of
this homomorphism to the Torelli group is twice the Johnson homomorphism
(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, (6.3)]</cite>, see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>).<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
            
            
          The
homomorphism induced by <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> was first constructed by Morita in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. The equality of Morita’s homomorphism and the one
induced by the normal function follows from results in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p22.1" class="ltx_p">Each of <math id="S1.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{3}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{3}H</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{3}H/H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{3}H/H</annotation></semantics></math> has an <math id="S1.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> invariant
skew symmetric bilinear form:</p>
<ol id="S1.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The form on <math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is the intersection pairing, <math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The form on <math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L:=\Lambda^{3}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L:=\Lambda^{3}H</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle x_{1}\wedge x_{2}\wedge x_{3},y_{1}\wedge y_{2}\wedge y_{3}\rangle=\det(x_{i},y_{j})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle x_{1}\wedge x_{2}\wedge x_{3},y_{1}\wedge y_{2}\wedge y_{3}\rangle=\det(x_{i},y_{j}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The form on <math id="S1.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L/H=\Lambda^{3}H/H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L/H=\Lambda^{3}H/H</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed in
Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Some Linear Algebra ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.p22.2" class="ltx_p">Each of these forms is primitive in the sense that it cannot be divided by
an integer and still be integer valued.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p23.1" class="ltx_p">Set</p>
<table id="S1.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{H}=\kappa^{\ast}\phi(H),\,\phi_{L}=\mu^{\ast}\phi(L)\text{ and }\phi_{L/H}=\nu^{\ast}\phi(L/H)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{H}=\kappa^{\ast}\phi(H),\,\phi_{L}=\mu^{\ast}\phi(L)\text{ and }\phi_{L/H}=\nu^{\ast}\phi(L/H).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p23.2" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S1.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(H)</annotation></semantics></math> equals the class <math id="S1.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> defined previously and that
<math id="S1.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{L/H}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{L/H}=0</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S1.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p24.1" class="ltx_p">The third result of Morita we wish to prove is:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Morita <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, (5.1)]</cite>)<span id="Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6><span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
          
          
        One should note that Morita’s class <math id="footnote4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> equals <math id="footnote4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\phi_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\phi_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> and
his class <math id="footnote4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> equals <math id="footnote4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\phi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\phi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. We will prove this in
Section <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7 (Morita [, (5.8)]). ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</span></span></span>
<div id="Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\omega}=\frac{1}{2g(2g+1)}(2\phi_{H}+3\phi_{L})\in H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\omega}=\frac{1}{2g(2g+1)}(2\phi_{H}+3\phi_{L})\in H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p25.1" class="ltx_p">By some easy computations in linear algebra (cf. Corollary <a href="#Thmtheorem19" title="Corollary 19. ‣ 5. Some Linear Algebra ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>)
we have</p>
<table id="S1.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g-1)\phi_{L}=\phi_{H}+\phi_{L/H}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g-1)\phi_{L}=\phi_{H}+\phi_{L/H}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p26.1" class="ltx_p">Combining this with Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Morita [, (1.7)]). ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, one arrives at the following
equivalent result which we shall prove in Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Proof of Theorem ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Morita <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, (5.8)]</cite>)<span id="Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\phi_{L/H}=(8g+4)\lambda\in H^{2}({\mathcal{M}}_{g},{\mathbb{Z}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\phi_{L/H}=(8g+4)\lambda\in H^{2}({\mathcal{M}}_{g},{\mathbb{Z}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 8</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S1.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}({\Gamma}_{g}^{n},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}({\Gamma}_{g}^{n},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> is torsion free when <math id="S1.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, there is no
torsion in <math id="S1.I4.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{n},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{n},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S1.I4.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. So if a relation between integral
cohomology classes holds in <math id="S1.I4.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{n},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{n},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds in <math id="S1.I4.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{n},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{n},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S1.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(8g+4)\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(8g+4)\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> appears frequently in identities involving divisor
classes on <math id="S1.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, such as in the work of Cornalba and Harris
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and Moriwaki <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. These papers are related to
Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7 (Morita [, (5.8)]). ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> as will be explained in a forthcoming
paper by the first author.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7 (Morita [, (5.8)]). ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> also gives a clean direct proof of
the non-triviality of the central extension in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p27" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p27.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p27.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Acknowledgements:</span> We would like to thank the referee
for making useful suggestions that lead to the simplification and
clarification of the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. In particular, the
statement of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 12. ‣ 2. Weierstrass Points ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> was suggested by the referee.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Weierstrass Points</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this section, <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a smooth projective curve of
genus <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. A point <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Weierstrass point</span> if
there is a non-constant rational function <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:C\to{\mathbb{P}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:C\to{\mathbb{P}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}(\infty)=P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(\infty)=P</annotation></semantics></math>. Equivalently, <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a Weierstrass point
if and only if <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(gP)&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(gP)&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. The multiplicity of <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> as a Weierstrass
point is <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(gP)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(gP)-1</annotation></semantics></math>. The Weierstrass point divisor of <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S2.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{C}=\sum_{P\in C}(h^{0}({\mathcal{O}}(gP)-1)P." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{C}=\sum_{P\in C}(h^{0}({\mathcal{O}}(gP)-1)P.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">It has degree <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(g-1)(g+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(g-1)(g+1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We shall denote
the group of divisor classes of degree <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}^{d}C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Pic</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}^{d}C</annotation></semantics></math> and the
<math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>th symmetric power of <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The divisor class map gives a
canonical morphism <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{d}:C^{(d)}\to\operatorname{Pic}^{d}C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Pic</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{d}:C^{(d)}\to\operatorname{Pic}^{d}C</annotation></semantics></math> whose image is the locus of
effective divisor classes of degree <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, which we shall denote by <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. The
map of <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is well known to be of degree 1 when <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq d\leq g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq d\leq g</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is classical that <math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is an imbedding. We shall identify <math id="S2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>
via <math id="S2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and denote <math id="S2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{g-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{g-1}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Define</p>
<table id="S2.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{C}:C\times C\to\operatorname{Pic}^{g-1}C" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Pic</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{C}:C\times C\to\operatorname{Pic}^{g-1}C</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">by <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{C}(P,Q)=gP-Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{C}(P,Q)=gP-Q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 9</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a divisor <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, with the same support as
<math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{C}^{\ast}\Theta_{C}=g\Delta+p_{1}^{\ast}E_{C}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{C}^{\ast}\Theta_{C}=g\Delta+p_{1}^{\ast}E_{C},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}:C\times C\to C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}:C\times C\to C</annotation></semantics></math> is projection onto the first factor.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, suppose that <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> are two distinct points of <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. If
<math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="gP-Q\in\Theta_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">gP-Q\in\Theta_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, then there exist <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1},\dots,P_{g-1}\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1},\dots,P_{g-1}\in C</annotation></semantics></math> such
that</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="gP\equiv Q+P_{1}+\dots+P_{g-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">gP\equiv Q+P_{1}+\dots+P_{g-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\neq Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\neq Q</annotation></semantics></math>, this implies that <math id="S2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}({\mathcal{O}}(gP))&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}({\mathcal{O}}(gP))&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and that <math id="S2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a
Weierstrass point. It follows that</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{C}^{\ast}\Theta_{C}=n\Delta+p_{1}^{\ast}E_{C}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{C}^{\ast}\Theta_{C}=n\Delta+p_{1}^{\ast}E_{C}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is a divisor in <math id="S2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> with the same support at <math id="S2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">To determine <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, pick a point <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> that is not a Weierstrass point.
Then, by the computation above, the image of <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}^{g-1}C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Pic</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}^{g-1}C</annotation></semantics></math> under the
mapping</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{P}:Q\mapsto gP-Q" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{P}:Q\mapsto gP-Q</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">intersects <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> only at <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g-1)P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g-1)P</annotation></semantics></math>. The Poincaré dual of <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is
the polarization</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}\wedge b_{1}+\dots+a_{g}\wedge b_{g}\in H^{2}(\operatorname{Pic}^{g-1}C,{\mathbb{Z}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Pic</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}\wedge b_{1}+\dots+a_{g}\wedge b_{g}\in H^{2}(\operatorname{Pic}^{g-1}C,{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},\dots,b_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},\dots,b_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a symplectic basis of <math id="S2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(C,{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(C,{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Integrating
this over the image of <math id="S2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that the degree of the pullback of
<math id="S2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> along <math id="S2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{P}^{\ast}\Theta_{C}=gP" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ν</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{P}^{\ast}\Theta_{C}=gP</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">and that <math id="S2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=g</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Define</p>
<table id="S2.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{C}:C\to\operatorname{Pic}^{g-1}C" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Pic</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{C}:C\to\operatorname{Pic}^{g-1}C</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">by <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{C}(P)=(g-1)P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{C}(P)=(g-1)P</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is just the restriction of <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> to
the diagonal in <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\times C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\times C</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the normal bundle of the diagonal
in <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\times C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\times C</annotation></semantics></math> is the tangent bundle of <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 10</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is an isomorphism of line bundles</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{C}^{\ast}{\mathcal{O}}(\Theta_{C})\cong{\boldsymbol{{\omega}}}_{C}^{\otimes(-g)}\otimes{\mathcal{O}}(E_{C}).\qed" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>C</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" lspace="0.0835em" mathvariant="italic" separator="true">∎</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{C}^{\ast}{\mathcal{O}}(\Theta_{C})\cong{\boldsymbol{{\omega}}}_{C}^{\otimes(-g)}\otimes{\mathcal{O}}(E_{C}).\qed</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 11</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg E_{C}=\deg W_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg E_{C}=\deg W_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The degree of <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{C}^{\ast}\Theta_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{C}^{\ast}\Theta_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is easily seen to be <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(g-1)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(g-1)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma 11. ‣ 2. Weierstrass Points ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, the degree of <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> equals the degree of
<math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(\Theta_{C})\otimes{\boldsymbol{{\omega}}}^{\otimes g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝝎</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(\Theta_{C})\otimes{\boldsymbol{{\omega}}}^{\otimes g}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g(g-1)+g(g-1)^{2}=g(g-1)(g+1)=\deg W_{C}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g(g-1)+g(g-1)^{2}=g(g-1)(g+1)=\deg W_{C}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We can now apply this to the universal curve <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have
the mapping</p>
<table id="S2.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2}\to\operatorname{Pic}^{g-1}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Pic</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2}\to\operatorname{Pic}^{g-1}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p8.2" class="ltx_p">whose restriction to the fiber over <math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[C]\in{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[C]\in{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote
the Weierstrass point divisor in <math id="S2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and the projection onto
the first factor by <math id="S2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. The universal theta divisor
in <math id="S2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}^{g-1}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Pic</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}^{g-1}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> will be denoted by <math id="S2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>. It has fiber
<math id="S2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[C]\in{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[C]\in{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 12</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">On <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\ast}\Theta=g\Delta+p_{1}^{\ast}W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\ast}\Theta=g\Delta+p_{1}^{\ast}W</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=\sigma^{\ast}\Theta-g\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=\sigma^{\ast}\Theta-g\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 9. ‣ 2. Weierstrass Points ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, the
support of <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> equals that of <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma 11. ‣ 2. Weierstrass Points ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, <math id="S2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>
have the same degree over <math id="S2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the Weierstrass points of the
generic curve are distinct, <math id="S2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> must be equal.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Restricting <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> to the diagonal copy of <math id="S2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we
obtain a morphism</p>
<table id="S2.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to\operatorname{Pic}^{g-1}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Pic</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to\operatorname{Pic}^{g-1}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p10.2" class="ltx_p">whose restriction to the fiber over <math id="S2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[C]\in{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[C]\in{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 13</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">As line bundles over <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\ast}{\mathcal{O}}(\Theta)\cong{\boldsymbol{{\omega}}}^{\otimes(-g)}\otimes{\mathcal{O}}(W)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝝎</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\ast}{\mathcal{O}}(\Theta)\cong{\boldsymbol{{\omega}}}^{\otimes(-g)}\otimes{\mathcal{O}}(W).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">This follows from the previous result and the following lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 14</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The normal bundle of <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> imbedded diagonally in <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}^{2}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}^{2}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is the relative
tangent bundle <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{{\omega}}}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝛚</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{{\omega}}}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the projection. The two projections of
<math id="S2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}^{2}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}^{2}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> induce a map <math id="S2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2}\to T{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\oplus T{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2}\to T{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\oplus T{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then have
an exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{CD}0@&gt;{}&gt;{}&gt;T{\mathcal{C}}_{g}@&gt;{\Delta}&gt;{}&gt;(T{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\oplus T{\mathcal{C}}_{g})|_{\text{diag}}@&gt;{f}&gt;{}&gt;T{\mathcal{C}}_{g}@&gt;{}&gt;{}&gt;T{\mathcal{M}}_{g}@&gt;{}&gt;{}&gt;0\end{CD}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mover class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy"><mo class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy">→</mo><mspace style="width:30.0pt;" width="30.0pt"></mspace></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover><mover class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy"><mo class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy">→</mo><mspace style="width:30.0pt;" width="30.0pt"></mspace></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mover></mtd><mtd><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mtext>diag</mtext></msub></mtd><mtd><mover><mover class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy"><mo class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy">→</mo><mspace style="width:30.0pt;" width="30.0pt"></mspace></mover><mi>f</mi></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy"><mo class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy">→</mo><mspace style="width:30.0pt;" width="30.0pt"></mspace></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy"><mo class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy">→</mo><mspace style="width:30.0pt;" width="30.0pt"></mspace></mover></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{CD}0@&gt;{}&gt;{}&gt;T{\mathcal{C}}_{g}@&gt;{\Delta}&gt;{}&gt;(T{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\oplus T{\mathcal{C}}_{g})|_{\text{diag}}@&gt;{f}&gt;{}&gt;T{\mathcal{C}}_{g}@&gt;{}&gt;{}&gt;T{\mathcal{M}}_{g}@&gt;{}&gt;{}&gt;0\end{CD}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is the diagonal map and <math id="S2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(v,w)=\pi_{\ast}(v)-\pi_{\ast}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(v,w)=\pi_{\ast}(v)-\pi_{\ast}(w)</annotation></semantics></math>.
It follows that the normal bundle is the kernel of <math id="S2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to T{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to T{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>,
which is the relative tangent bundle.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will prove Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> by comparing the formula for the class of
Weierstrass point locus <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> derived in the previous section with a second
expression for the class of <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> derived using Wronskians. This second
expression is due to Arakelov <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Lemma 3.3]</cite> as was pointed out
to us by Jean-Benoit Bost.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 15</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Arakelov)<span id="Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Over <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}({\mathcal{O}}(W))=\binom{g+1}{2}w-\lambda." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}({\mathcal{O}}(W))=\binom{g+1}{2}w-\lambda.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose that <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{1}(t),\dots,\zeta_{g}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{1}(t),\dots,\zeta_{g}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is a local holomorphic framing
of the Hodge bundle <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\ast}\Omega^{1}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{g}/{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\ast}\Omega^{1}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{g}/{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(\zeta_{1},\dots,\zeta_{g})\otimes(\zeta_{1}\wedge\dots\wedge\zeta_{g})^{-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(\zeta_{1},\dots,\zeta_{g})\otimes(\zeta_{1}\wedge\dots\wedge\zeta_{g})^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">is a section of <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{{\omega}}}^{\otimes\binom{g+1}{2}}\otimes{\mathcal{L}}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝝎</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{{\omega}}}^{\otimes\binom{g+1}{2}}\otimes{\mathcal{L}}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> that is independent
of the choice of the framing, and therefore extends to a global section over
<math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(\zeta_{1},\dots,\zeta_{g}):=\left|\begin{matrix}f_{1}(t,z)&amp;\dots&amp;f_{g}(t,z)\cr\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\cr\frac{\partial^{g-1}}{\partial z^{g-1}}f_{1}(t,z)&amp;\dots&amp;\frac{\partial^{g-1}}{\partial z^{g-1}}f_{g}(t,z)\cr\end{matrix}\right|dz^{\binom{g+1}{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(\zeta_{1},\dots,\zeta_{g}):=\left|\begin{matrix}f_{1}(t,z)&amp;\dots&amp;f_{g}(t,z)\cr\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\cr\frac{\partial^{g-1}}{\partial z^{g-1}}f_{1}(t,z)&amp;\dots&amp;\frac{\partial^{g-1}}{\partial z^{g-1}}f_{g}(t,z)\cr\end{matrix}\right|dz^{\binom{g+1}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">is the Wronskian, where <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{j}(t)=f_{j}(t,z)dz" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{j}(t)=f_{j}(t,z)dz</annotation></semantics></math> locally. It is a
section of <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{{\omega}}}^{\otimes\binom{g+1}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝝎</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{{\omega}}}^{\otimes\binom{g+1}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The result follows as <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is the
divisor of this section by classical Riemann surface theory.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Choose a theta characteristic <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> defined over <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}[2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}[2]</annotation></semantics></math>. This gives
an isomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{CD}\operatorname{Pic}^{g-1}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}@&gt;{\alpha}&gt;{}&gt;\operatorname{Jac}_{/{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\end{CD}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>Pic</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover><mover class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy"><mo class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy">→</mo><mspace style="width:30.0pt;" width="30.0pt"></mspace></mover><mi>α</mi></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>Jac</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{CD}\operatorname{Pic}^{g-1}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}@&gt;{\alpha}&gt;{}&gt;\operatorname{Jac}_{/{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\end{CD}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">The composition of <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> with this mapping is the mapping</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{\alpha}:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{J}}=\operatorname{Jac}_{/{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Jac</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{\alpha}:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{J}}=\operatorname{Jac}_{/{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">in the introduction.
Denote the image of <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> in the universal jacobian by <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It follows from Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem13" title="Theorem 13. ‣ 2. Weierstrass Points ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[W]=j_{\alpha}^{\ast}\theta+gw\in H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g}[2],{\mathbb{Q}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[W]=j_{\alpha}^{\ast}\theta+gw\in H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g}[2],{\mathbb{Q}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">On the other hand, Arakelov’s computation implies that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[W]=\binom{g+1}{2}w-\lambda." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[W]=\binom{g+1}{2}w-\lambda.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.5.1" class="ltx_text">Equating these two expressions, we see that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{\alpha}^{\ast}\theta=\binom{g}{2}w-\lambda." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{\alpha}^{\ast}\theta=\binom{g}{2}w-\lambda.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.6.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Proposition <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">First note that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}({\mathcal{J}}[2],{\mathbb{Q}})\cong H^{\bullet}(Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})[2]\ltimes H_{\mathbb{Z}},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋉</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}({\mathcal{J}}[2],{\mathbb{Q}})\cong H^{\bullet}(Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})[2]\ltimes H_{\mathbb{Z}},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">By a theorem of Raghunathan <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(Sp_{g}[2],H_{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(Sp_{g}[2],H_{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>
vanishes and, by a theorem of Borel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})[2],{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})[2],{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>
has rank one when <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is not zero in <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\mathcal{J}}[2],{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\mathcal{J}}[2],{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows, by looking at the
Hochschild-Serre spectral sequence of the group extension</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to H_{\mathbb{Z}}\to Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})[2]\ltimes H_{\mathbb{Z}}\to Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})[2]\to 1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋉</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to H_{\mathbb{Z}}\to Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})[2]\ltimes H_{\mathbb{Z}}\to Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})[2]\to 1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">that <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\mathcal{J}}[2],{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\mathcal{J}}[2],{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> is two dimensional and spanned by <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>.
By restricting the class <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> to any fiber of <math id="S3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}[2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}[2]</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=\phi+c\lambda." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=\phi+c\lambda.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">To determine the rational number <math id="S3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, we restrict <math id="S3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> to the zero section
of <math id="S3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}[2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}[2]</annotation></semantics></math>. If we choose <math id="S3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> to be even (i.e., <math id="S3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϑ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is an
even theta function), then we see that the restriction of <math id="S3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> to the
zero section is the divisor corresponding to the theta null corresponding
to <math id="S3.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϑ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the theta nulls are sections of a square root
of the determinant <math id="S3.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the Hodge bundle, as follows from the
transformation law for theta nulls, we see
that <math id="S3.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=1/2</annotation></semantics></math>. The proof in the case when <math id="S3.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> is simpler and left to the
reader.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4 (Morita [, (1.3)]). ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This result is not nearly as deep as the previous result. It follows from
standard computations when <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> that <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> is four dimensional
and has basis <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, the dual of the
diagonally imbedded copy of <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{\ast}(\phi)=a\lambda+b\psi_{1}+c\psi_{2}+d\Delta" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{\ast}(\phi)=a\lambda+b\psi_{1}+c\psi_{2}+d\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the coefficients are rational numbers. It follows from
Proposition <a href="#Thmtheorem14" title="Lemma 14. ‣ 2. Weierstrass Points ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> that the restriction of <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> to the diagonal is
<math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\psi</annotation></semantics></math>. So the restriction of <math id="S4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{\ast}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{\ast}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> to the diagonal is</p>
<table id="S4.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\lambda+(b+c-d)\psi\in H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\lambda+(b+c-d)\psi\in H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The image of the restriction of <math id="S4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2}\to{\mathcal{J}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta:{\mathcal{C}}_{g}^{2}\to{\mathcal{J}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the diagonal
<math id="S4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> has image contained in the zero section of <math id="S4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the
restriction of <math id="S4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> to the zero section of <math id="S4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial, it follows that
<math id="S4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\lambda+(b+c-d)\psi=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\lambda+(b+c-d)\psi=0</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b+c-d=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b+c-d=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
The constants <math id="S4.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> can now be determined by restricting to any
fiber <math id="S4.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\times C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\times C</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, it is an elementary exercise
in algebraic topology to show that under the difference map, <math id="S4.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> pulls
back to <math id="S4.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta+(\psi_{1}+\psi_{2})/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta+(\psi_{1}+\psi_{2})/2</annotation></semantics></math>. The result follows.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Some Linear Algebra</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As in the introduction, we denote the fundamental representation of
<math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and the standard symplectic form on <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote its third exterior power <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{3}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{3}H</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and the natural
symplectic form on it, which was defined in the introduction, by <math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},\dots,a_{g},b_{1},\dots,b_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},\dots,a_{g},b_{1},\dots,b_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a symplectic basis of <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, then the
element <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta=\sum_{j=1}^{g}a_{j}\wedge b_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>g</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta=\sum_{j=1}^{g}a_{j}\wedge b_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> invariant
element of <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{2}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{2}H</annotation></semantics></math> which is independent of the choice of the basis.
Wedging with <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> induces an <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> invariant mapping <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\to L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\to L</annotation></semantics></math>.
One also has the contraction map <math id="S5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c:L\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c:L\to H</annotation></semantics></math> which is defined by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c:x\wedge y\wedge z\mapsto q_{H}(x,y)z+q_{H}(y,z)x+q_{H}(z,x)y." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c:x\wedge y\wedge z\mapsto q_{H}(x,y)z+q_{H}(y,z)x+q_{H}(z,x)y.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 16</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">When <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, the composite <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\to L\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\to L\to H</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g-1</annotation></semantics></math> times the identity.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 17</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">When <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c:L\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c:L\to H</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism. Consequently, <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L/H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L/H</annotation></semantics></math> is zero
when <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Denote the image in <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L/H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L/H</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\wedge y\wedge z\in L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\wedge y\wedge z\in L</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{x\wedge y\wedge z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{x\wedge y\wedge z}</annotation></semantics></math>. The projection <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:L\to L/H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:L\to L/H</annotation></semantics></math> has a
canonical <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> equivariant splitting <math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> after tensoring with <math id="S5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is defined by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j(\overline{x\wedge y\wedge z})=x\wedge y\wedge z-\zeta\wedge c(x\wedge y\wedge z)/(g-1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j(\overline{x\wedge y\wedge z})=x\wedge y\wedge z-\zeta\wedge c(x\wedge y\wedge z)/(g-1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">An <math id="S5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> invariant symplectic form on <math id="S5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L/H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L/H</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{L/H}(u,v)=(g-1)q_{L}(j(u),j(v))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{L/H}(u,v)=(g-1)q_{L}(j(u),j(v)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p3.3" class="ltx_p">One can easily check that this form is integral and primitive. The following
result follows from a straightforward computation.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem18.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 18</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem18.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem18.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="Thmtheorem18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="Thmtheorem18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g-1)q_{L}=p^{\ast}q_{L/H}+c^{\ast}q_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g-1)q_{L}=p^{\ast}q_{L/H}+c^{\ast}q_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 19</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem19.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem19.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The classes <math id="Thmtheorem19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="Thmtheorem19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{L/H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{L/H}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmtheorem19.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\mathcal{C}}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, defined in
the introduction, satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g-1)\phi_{L}=\phi_{L/H}+\phi_{H}\qed" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" lspace="0em" mathvariant="italic" separator="true">∎</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g-1)\phi_{L}=\phi_{L/H}+\phi_{H}\qed</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7 (Morita [, (5.8)]). ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a></h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by dispensing with the cases <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. In these cases
<math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(8g+4)\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(8g+4)\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> is cyclic of order <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, as observed in Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Some Linear Algebra ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>,
<math id="S6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L/H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L/H</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial in these cases too. So both <math id="S6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(8g+4)\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(8g+4)\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{L/H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{L/H}</annotation></semantics></math>
vanish and the result is trivially true.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Now suppose that <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. We shall denote the mapping class group of a compact
oriented surface <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> boundary components and <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> distinct
marked points (not lying on the boundary of <math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>) by <math id="S6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{g,r}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{g,r}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. As usual, <math id="S6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> are omitted when they are zero. Here we consider only <math id="S6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S6.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The natural homomorphisms</p>
<table id="S6.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{g,1}\to{\Gamma}_{g}^{1}\to{\Gamma}_{g}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{g,1}\to{\Gamma}_{g}^{1}\to{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">induce homomorphisms</p>
<table id="S6.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},{\mathbb{Z}})\hookrightarrow H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{1},{\mathbb{Z}})\twoheadrightarrow H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g,1},{\mathbb{Z}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↠</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},{\mathbb{Z}})\hookrightarrow H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{1},{\mathbb{Z}})\twoheadrightarrow H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g,1},{\mathbb{Z}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Harer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> has proved that the left and right hand groups have
rank one and are generated by <math id="S6.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. The middle group
has rank two and is generated by <math id="S6.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> as
can be seen by applying the Gysin sequence to the group extension</p>
<table id="S6.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to{\mathbb{Z}}\to{\Gamma}_{g,1}\to{\Gamma}_{g}^{1}\to 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to{\mathbb{Z}}\to{\Gamma}_{g,1}\to{\Gamma}_{g}^{1}\to 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Since these groups are torsion free when <math id="S6.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. Remark <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Remark 8. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>),
and since <math id="S6.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\phi_{L/H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\phi_{L/H}</annotation></semantics></math> is integral, it suffices to show that
<math id="S6.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{L/H}=(4g+2)\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{L/H}=(4g+2)\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{1},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{1},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We know that</p>
<table id="S6.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{L/H}=x\lambda\in H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{L/H}=x\lambda\in H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p3.2" class="ltx_p">for some <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>. To determine <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, we work in <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{1},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{1},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Morita [, (1.7)]). ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>,</p>
<table id="S6.Ex60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{H}=2g(1-g)\psi-6\lambda\in H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{1},{\mathbb{Q}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{H}=2g(1-g)\psi-6\lambda\in H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{1},{\mathbb{Q}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p3.3" class="ltx_p">so that <math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6\phi_{L/H}-x\phi_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6\phi_{L/H}-x\phi_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is a multiple of <math id="S6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To compute the constant <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> we convert the problem into linear algebra.
Denote the Lie algebra of the prounipotent radical of the completion
of <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{g,r}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{g,r}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the standard representation
<math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{g,r}^{n}\to Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{g,r}^{n}\to Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{u}}_{g,r}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔲</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{u}}_{g,r}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. (See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> for
definitions.) There is a natural homomorphism</p>
<table id="S6.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,r}^{n})^{Sp_{g}}\to H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g,r}^{n},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔲</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,r}^{n})^{Sp_{g}}\to H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g,r}^{n},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p4.2" class="ltx_p">which is an isomorphism when <math id="S6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
            
            
          One can see this by
first reducing to the case where <math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=n=0</annotation></semantics></math> using the exact sequence
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, (3.6)]</cite>, the result <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, (8.2)]</cite>,
and a spectral sequence argument. The case <math id="footnote5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=n=0</annotation></semantics></math> can then be proved
using <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> for the computation of <math id="footnote5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, (7.1)]</cite> for the injectivity of the homomorphism, and
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, (11.1)]</cite> to see that <math id="footnote5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g})^{Sp_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔲</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g})^{Sp_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math> is one
dimensional.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Since the kernel of <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{1},{\mathbb{Q}})\to H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g,1},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{1},{\mathbb{Q}})\to H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g,1},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> is spanned by
<math id="S6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that</p>
<table id="S6.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}\,\psi=\ker\{H^{2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g}^{1})^{Sp_{g}}\to H^{2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1})^{Sp_{g}}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ker</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔲</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔲</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}\,\psi=\ker\{H^{2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g}^{1})^{Sp_{g}}\to H^{2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1})^{Sp_{g}}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p5.2" class="ltx_p">To determine the combinations of <math id="S6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{L/H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{L/H}</annotation></semantics></math> that lie in the
kernel of this map we use the short exact sequence</p>
<table id="S6.Ex63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{CD}0@&gt;{}&gt;{}&gt;\operatorname{Gr}^{W}_{-2}H_{2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1})@&gt;{\text{cup}^{\ast}}&gt;{}&gt;\Lambda^{2}H_{1}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1})@&gt;{\beta}&gt;{}&gt;\operatorname{Gr}^{W}_{-2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1})@&gt;{}&gt;{}&gt;0.\end{CD}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mover class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy"><mo class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy">→</mo><mspace style="width:30.0pt;" width="30.0pt"></mspace></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔲</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover><mover class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy"><mo class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy">→</mo><mspace style="width:30.0pt;" width="30.0pt"></mspace></mover><msup><mtext>cup</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msup></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔲</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover><mover class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy"><mo class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy">→</mo><mspace style="width:30.0pt;" width="30.0pt"></mspace></mover><mi>β</mi></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔲</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy"><mo class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy">→</mo><mspace style="width:30.0pt;" width="30.0pt"></mspace></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{CD}0@&gt;{}&gt;{}&gt;\operatorname{Gr}^{W}_{-2}H_{2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1})@&gt;{\text{cup}^{\ast}}&gt;{}&gt;\Lambda^{2}H_{1}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1})@&gt;{\beta}&gt;{}&gt;\operatorname{Gr}^{W}_{-2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1})@&gt;{}&gt;{}&gt;0.\end{CD}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p5.3" class="ltx_p">The first map is the dual of the cup product and <math id="S6.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is induced by
the bracket. Taking invariants, we obtain the exact sequence</p>
<table id="S6.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{CD}0\longrightarrow\operatorname{Gr}^{W}_{-2}H_{2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1})^{Sp_{g}}@&gt;{\text{cup}^{\ast}}&gt;{}&gt;\Lambda^{2}H_{1}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1})^{Sp_{g}}@&gt;{\beta}&gt;{}&gt;\operatorname{Gr}^{W}_{-2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1})^{Sp_{g}}\longrightarrow 0\end{CD}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔲</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover><mover class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy"><mo class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy">→</mo><mspace style="width:30.0pt;" width="30.0pt"></mspace></mover><msup><mtext>cup</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msup></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔲</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover><mover class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy"><mo class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy">→</mo><mspace style="width:30.0pt;" width="30.0pt"></mspace></mover><mi>β</mi></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔲</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{CD}0\longrightarrow\operatorname{Gr}^{W}_{-2}H_{2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1})^{Sp_{g}}@&gt;{\text{cup}^{\ast}}&gt;{}&gt;\Lambda^{2}H_{1}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1})^{Sp_{g}}@&gt;{\beta}&gt;{}&gt;\operatorname{Gr}^{W}_{-2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1})^{Sp_{g}}\longrightarrow 0\end{CD}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p5.4" class="ltx_p">Denote the fundamental representation of <math id="S6.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S6.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows from
Johnson’s Theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> that the Johnson homomorphism induces
an <math id="S6.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> equivariant isomorphism</p>
<table id="S6.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1})\cong\Lambda^{3}H." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔲</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1})\cong\Lambda^{3}H.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p5.5" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},\dots,a_{g},b_{1},\dots,b_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},\dots,a_{g},b_{1},\dots,b_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic basis of <math id="S6.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S6.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1},\dots,A_{d},B_{1},\dots,B_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1},\dots,A_{d},B_{1},\dots,B_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic basis of the kernel
of the contraction <math id="S6.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c:\Lambda^{3}H\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c:\Lambda^{3}H\to H</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the symplectic
form <math id="S6.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{3}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{3}H</annotation></semantics></math>, where</p>
<table id="S6.Ex66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d=\dim\Lambda^{3}H/H=\binom{2g}{3}-2g=\frac{2g(2g+1)(g-2)}{3}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d=\dim\Lambda^{3}H/H=\binom{2g}{3}-2g=\frac{2g(2g+1)(g-2)}{3}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p5.6" class="ltx_p">Note that this is where the <math id="S6.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8g+4=4(2g+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8g+4=4(2g+1)</annotation></semantics></math> in the statement of the theorem
will come from. Set</p>
<table id="S6.Ex67" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta=\sum_{j=1}^{g}a_{j}\wedge b_{j}\in\left(\Lambda^{2}H\right)^{Sp_{g}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>g</mi></munderover><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta=\sum_{j=1}^{g}a_{j}\wedge b_{j}\in\left(\Lambda^{2}H\right)^{Sp_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p5.7" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S6.Ex68" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{H}=\sum_{j=1}^{g}a_{j}\wedge\zeta\bigwedge b_{j}\wedge\zeta\text{ and }f_{L/H}=\sum_{j=1}^{d}A_{j}\bigwedge B_{j}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>g</mi></munderover><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⋀</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⋀</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{H}=\sum_{j=1}^{g}a_{j}\wedge\zeta\bigwedge b_{j}\wedge\zeta\text{ and }f_{L/H}=\sum_{j=1}^{d}A_{j}\bigwedge B_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p5.8" class="ltx_p">which are elements of <math id="S6.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\Lambda^{2}\Lambda^{3}H\right)^{Sp_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\Lambda^{2}\Lambda^{3}H\right)^{Sp_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math> and
are viewed as elements of <math id="S6.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{2}H_{1}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1})^{Sp_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔲</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{2}H_{1}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1})^{Sp_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math> via the
isomorphism above.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Denote the Malcev Lie algebra of the fundamental group of a surface with one
boundary component by <math id="S6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{p}}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔭</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{p}}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math>. (It is isomorphic to the free pronilpotent
Lie algebra generated by <math id="S6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.) View <math id="S6.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> as the element <math id="S6.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j}[a_{j},b_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j}[a_{j},b_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S6.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Gr}^{W}_{-2}{\mathfrak{p}}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔭</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Gr}^{W}_{-2}{\mathfrak{p}}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The canonical map</p>
<table id="S6.Ex69" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:\operatorname{Gr}^{W}_{-2}{\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1}\to\operatorname{Gr}^{W}_{-2}\operatorname{Der}{\mathfrak{p}}_{g,1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔲</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Der</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔭</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:\operatorname{Gr}^{W}_{-2}{\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1}\to\operatorname{Gr}^{W}_{-2}\operatorname{Der}{\mathfrak{p}}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p6.2" class="ltx_p">is injective.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">It follows from a direct computation using <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, §11]</cite>
or the fact that the Euler class of a surface of genus <math id="S6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2-2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2-2g</annotation></semantics></math> that</p>
<table id="S6.Ex70" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\circ\beta(f_{H})=(2-2g)\operatorname{ad}(\zeta)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ad</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\circ\beta(f_{H})=(2-2g)\operatorname{ad}(\zeta),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p7.2" class="ltx_p">and from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, (11.4)]</cite> that</p>
<table id="S6.Ex71" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex71.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alpha\circ\beta(f_{L/H})=-6\,\sum_{j=1}^{d}\frac{q_{L/H}(A_{j},B_{j})}{g(2g+1)}\operatorname{ad}(\zeta)=\frac{-6d}{g(2g+1)}\operatorname{ad}(\zeta)=-4(g-2)\operatorname{ad}(\zeta)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>6</mn><munderover><mo lspace="0.337em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mi>ad</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mi>ad</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>4</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ad</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\circ\beta(f_{L/H})=-6\,\sum_{j=1}^{d}\frac{q_{L/H}(A_{j},B_{j})}{g(2g+1)}\operatorname{ad}(\zeta)=\frac{-6d}{g(2g+1)}\operatorname{ad}(\zeta)=-4(g-2)\operatorname{ad}(\zeta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p7.3" class="ltx_p">Consequently</p>
<table id="S6.Ex72" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\circ\beta\left(2(g-2)f_{H}-(g-1)f_{L/H}\right)=0\text{ in }\operatorname{Gr}^{W}_{-2}\operatorname{Der}{\mathfrak{p}}_{g,1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> in </mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Der</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔭</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\circ\beta\left(2(g-2)f_{H}-(g-1)f_{L/H}\right)=0\text{ in }\operatorname{Gr}^{W}_{-2}\operatorname{Der}{\mathfrak{p}}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p7.4" class="ltx_p">and therefore <math id="S6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2(g-2)f_{H}-(g-1)f_{L/H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2(g-2)f_{H}-(g-1)f_{L/H}</annotation></semantics></math> spans the one dimensional
vector space <math id="S6.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1})^{Sp_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔲</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g,1})^{Sp_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently</p>
<table id="S6.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle 6\phi_{L/H}+x\phi_{H},2(g-2)f_{H}-(g-1)f_{L/H}\rangle=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle 6\phi_{L/H}+x\phi_{H},2(g-2)f_{H}-(g-1)f_{L/H}\rangle=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Now <math id="S6.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{L}\in H^{2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g}^{1})^{Sp_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔲</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{L}\in H^{2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g}^{1})^{Sp_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math> takes the value</p>
<table id="S6.Ex73" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c^{\ast}q_{H})(f_{H})=(g-1)^{2}q_{H}(\zeta)=g(g-1)^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c^{\ast}q_{H})(f_{H})=(g-1)^{2}q_{H}(\zeta)=g(g-1)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p8.2" class="ltx_p">on <math id="S6.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S6.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{L/H}\in H^{2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g}^{1})^{Sp_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔲</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{L/H}\in H^{2}({\mathfrak{u}}_{g}^{1})^{Sp_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math> takes the value</p>
<table id="S6.Ex74" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p^{\ast}q_{L/H})(f_{L/H})=(g-1)q_{L}(f_{L/H})=d(g-1)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p^{\ast}q_{L/H})(f_{L/H})=(g-1)q_{L}(f_{L/H})=d(g-1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p8.3" class="ltx_p">on <math id="S6.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{L/H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{L/H}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S6.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{H}(f_{L/H})=\phi_{L/H}(f_{H})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{H}(f_{L/H})=\phi_{L/H}(f_{H})=0</annotation></semantics></math> (by representation
theory, for example), (<a href="#S6.E1" title="In 6. Proof of Theorem ‣ Geometric Proofs of Some Results of Morita" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) becomes</p>
<table id="S6.Ex75" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex75.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(g-2)(g-1)^{2}x-6d(g-1)^{2}=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(g-2)(g-1)^{2}x-6d(g-1)^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p8.4" class="ltx_p">from which it follows that <math id="S6.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=4g+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=4g+2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem20.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 20</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem20.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We give a brief explanation of why Morita’s classes <math id="Thmtheorem20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem20.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> defined
in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, p. 173]</cite> are related to ours by</p>
<table id="S6.Ex76" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1}=2\phi_{H}\text{ and }z_{2}=3\phi_{L}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1}=2\phi_{H}\text{ and }z_{2}=3\phi_{L}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem20.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The first point is that his forms <math id="Thmtheorem20.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem20.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are related to ours by</p>
<table id="S6.Ex77" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex77.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}=4c^{\ast}q(H)\text{ and }C_{2}=6q(L)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}=4c^{\ast}q(H)\text{ and }C_{2}=6q(L).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem20.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This can be seen by direct computation.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem20.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem20.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The second point is that Morita uses these quadratic forms to construct
extensions of <math id="Thmtheorem20.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\Lambda^{3}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\Lambda^{3}H</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmtheorem20.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas we are considering
extensions of <math id="Thmtheorem20.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{3}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{3}H</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmtheorem20.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The isomorphism</p>
<table id="S6.Ex78" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex78.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\text{multiplication by }2):\frac{1}{2}\Lambda^{3}H\to\Lambda^{3}H" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>multiplication by </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\text{multiplication by }2):\frac{1}{2}\Lambda^{3}H\to\Lambda^{3}H</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem20.p2.2" class="ltx_p">multiplies quadratic forms by 4. So if we rewrite Morita’s proof using the
group</p>
<table id="S6.Ex79" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex79.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\ltimes\Lambda^{3}H\text{ instead of }Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\ltimes\frac{1}{2}\Lambda^{3}H" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋉</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> instead of </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋉</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\ltimes\Lambda^{3}H\text{ instead of }Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\ltimes\frac{1}{2}\Lambda^{3}H</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem20.p2.3" class="ltx_p">we need to replace <math id="Thmtheorem20.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmtheorem20.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}/4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem20.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmtheorem20.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}/4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem20.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem20.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The final point is that Morita’s construction of an extension of
<math id="Thmtheorem20.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{3}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{3}H</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmtheorem20.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> from a skew symmetric form <math id="Thmtheorem20.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> (given at the top
of page 174 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>) yields one whose Chern class
is twice the cohomology class in <math id="Thmtheorem20.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\Lambda^{3}H,{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\Lambda^{3}H,{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to <math id="Thmtheorem20.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.
This may be seen by computing commutators in the corresponding Heisenberg
groups.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem20.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem20.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Putting all this together, we have <math id="Thmtheorem20.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1}=2(4\phi_{H}/4)=2\phi_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1}=2(4\phi_{H}/4)=2\phi_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmtheorem20.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{2}=2(6\phi_{L}/4)=3\phi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{2}=2(6\phi_{L}/4)=3\phi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Remarks on <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, (5.4)]</cite></h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This result of Morita states that if <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="S7.Ex80" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex80.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Z}})\cong{\mathbb{Z}}/(2g-2){\mathbb{Z}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Z}})\cong{\mathbb{Z}}/(2g-2){\mathbb{Z}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> has an <math id="S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> invariant unimodular form, it is isomorphic
to its dual as a <math id="S7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> module. So, by the universal coefficient theorem,</p>
<table id="S7.Ex81" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex81.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{torsion subgroup of }H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Z}})\cong{\mathbb{Z}}/(2g-2){\mathbb{Z}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>torsion subgroup of </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{torsion subgroup of }H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Z}})\cong{\mathbb{Z}}/(2g-2){\mathbb{Z}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem21.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 21</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem21.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem21.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="Thmtheorem21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Harer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> has shown that the natural homomorphism
<math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{g,1}\to{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{g,1}\to{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> induces an isomorphism on <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>
coefficients for all <math id="S7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. One can now use the Gysin sequence</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex82" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex82.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to H^{0}({\Gamma}_{g,1},{\mathbb{Q}})\to H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{1},{\mathbb{Q}})\to H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g,1},{\mathbb{Q}})\to H^{1}({\Gamma}_{g}^{1},{\mathbb{Q}})\dots" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to H^{0}({\Gamma}_{g,1},{\mathbb{Q}})\to H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{1},{\mathbb{Q}})\to H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g,1},{\mathbb{Q}})\to H^{1}({\Gamma}_{g}^{1},{\mathbb{Q}})\dots</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">and the fact that the second Betti number of <math id="S7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is 0 in genus 2
and 1 when <math id="S7.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> to show that</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex83" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex83.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{1},{\mathbb{Q}})=1+\dim H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})=\begin{cases}1&amp;g=2;\cr 2&amp;g\geq 3.\cr\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{1},{\mathbb{Q}})=1+\dim H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})=\begin{cases}1&amp;g=2;\cr 2&amp;g\geq 3.\cr\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">Now consider the Hochschild-Serre spectral sequence of the group
extension</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex84" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex84.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\to\pi_{1}(\text{reference surface},*)\to{\Gamma}_{g}^{1}\to{\Gamma}_{g}\to 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>reference surface</mtext><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\to\pi_{1}(\text{reference surface},*)\to{\Gamma}_{g}^{1}\to{\Gamma}_{g}\to 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">By passing to subgroups of level <math id="S7.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> and applying Deligne’s
degeneration result <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, we see that this spectral
sequence (for cohomology with <math id="S7.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> coefficients) degenerates at
the <math id="S7.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> term. This implies that</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex85" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex85.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{1},{\mathbb{Q}})=1+\dim H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})+\dim H^{1}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Q}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}^{1},{\mathbb{Q}})=1+\dim H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},{\mathbb{Q}})+\dim H^{1}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Q}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p2.5.1" class="ltx_text">It follows that <math id="S7.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes for all <math id="S7.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. The
result is easily proved when <math id="S7.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> using the “center kills trick”
— <math id="S7.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-I</annotation></semantics></math>, being central in <math id="S7.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{1}=SL_{2}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{1}=SL_{2}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> acts
trivially on <math id="S7.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}({\Gamma}_{1},H_{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}({\Gamma}_{1},H_{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> on the one hand, and as <math id="S7.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-I</annotation></semantics></math> on the
other as it acts this way in <math id="S7.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, which forces <math id="S7.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(SL_{2}({\mathbb{Z}}),H_{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(SL_{2}({\mathbb{Z}}),H_{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>
to be trivial.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Combining this with Morita’s computation, we obtain:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem22.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 22</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem22.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem22.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Z}})\cong{\mathbb{Z}}/(2g-2){\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Z}})\cong{\mathbb{Z}}/(2g-2){\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This result has a natural geometric interpretation: <math id="S7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(G,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(G,A)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
is a group and <math id="S7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-module, classifies extensions of <math id="S7.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S7.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>; the
identity being the split extension <math id="S7.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\ltimes A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋉</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\ltimes A</annotation></semantics></math>. So Morita’s result says
that when <math id="S7.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, the set of equivalence classes of central extensions
of <math id="S7.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S7.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a cyclic group of order <math id="S7.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2</annotation></semantics></math>. It is easy to
realize these extensions geometrically.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> one has the degree <math id="S7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> part <math id="S7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}^{d}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Pic</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}^{d}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
of the relative Picard group of the universal curve. Since the base and fiber
of this map are Eilenberg-MacLane spaces <math id="S7.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\pi,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\pi,1)</annotation></semantics></math> (in the orbifold sense),
the total space is too, and we have a short exact sequence of fundamental
groups</p>
<table id="S7.Ex86" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex86.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to H_{\mathbb{Z}}\to\pi_{1}(\operatorname{Pic}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}^{d}{\mathcal{C}}_{g},*)\to{\Gamma}_{g}\to 1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Pic</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to H_{\mathbb{Z}}\to\pi_{1}(\operatorname{Pic}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}^{d}{\mathcal{C}}_{g},*)\to{\Gamma}_{g}\to 1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p5.2" class="ltx_p">as, for each curve <math id="S7.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a canonical isomorphism
<math id="S7.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}(\operatorname{Pic}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}^{d}{\mathcal{C}}_{g})\cong H_{1}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Pic</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}(\operatorname{Pic}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}^{d}{\mathcal{C}}_{g})\cong H_{1}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking <math id="S7.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> to the class of this
extension in <math id="S7.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>, one obtains a function</p>
<table id="S7.Ex87" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex87.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon:{\mathbb{Z}}\to H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Z}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon:{\mathbb{Z}}\to H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Z}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p5.3" class="ltx_p">Using the addition map</p>
<table id="S7.Ex88" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex88.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}^{d}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\times_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}\operatorname{Pic}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}^{e}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to\operatorname{Pic}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}^{d+e}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Pic</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>Pic</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>e</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Pic</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}^{d}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\times_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}\operatorname{Pic}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}^{e}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to\operatorname{Pic}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}^{d+e}{\mathcal{C}}_{g},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p5.4" class="ltx_p">it is easy to show this function is a group homomorphism. The canonical
bundle gives a section of <math id="S7.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}^{2g-2}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Pic</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}^{2g-2}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, this
homomorphism factors through <math id="S7.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}/(2g-2){\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}/(2g-2){\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem23" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem23.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 23</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem23.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem23.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem23.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="Thmtheorem23.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem23.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon(d)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon(d)=0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="Thmtheorem23.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is divisible by <math id="Thmtheorem23.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We have already proved that if <math id="S7.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is divisible by <math id="S7.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="S7.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon(d)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon(d)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="S7.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;d\leq 2g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;d\leq 2g-2</annotation></semantics></math> generates <math id="S7.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S7.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d|(2g-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d|(2g-2)</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S7.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=(2g-2)/d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=(2g-2)/d</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S7.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon(d)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon(d)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a
smooth section <math id="S7.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S7.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}^{d}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Pic</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}^{d}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S7.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\cdot s-(\text{canonical bundle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>canonical bundle</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\cdot s-(\text{canonical bundle})</annotation></semantics></math>
is a smooth section of the universal jacobian and therefore determines a
cohomology class in <math id="S7.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows from Johnson’s theorems
that this group is torsion (see, for example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, (5.2)]</cite>.)
By the universal coefficient theorem, <math id="S7.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Z}})\cong H_{0}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Z}})\cong H_{0}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
which is trivial. It follows that the class of the section
<math id="S7.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\cdot s-(\text{canonical bundle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>canonical bundle</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\cdot s-(\text{canonical bundle})</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial, and therefore that
<math id="S7.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is homotopic to a section of <math id="S7.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}^{d}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Pic</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}^{d}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding
to an <math id="S7.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> th root of the canonical bundle. But by the solution
to the Francetta Conjecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> (see also
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, §12]</cite>), this implies that <math id="S7.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is divisible by <math id="S7.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2</annotation></semantics></math>,
and therefore that <math id="S7.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2g-2</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Combining this with Morita’s Theorem, we obtain:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem24" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem24.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 24</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem24.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem24.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem24.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="Thmtheorem24.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, then the map <math id="Thmtheorem24.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}/(2g-2){\mathbb{Z}}\to H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}/(2g-2){\mathbb{Z}}\to H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> that takes <math id="Thmtheorem24.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>
to the class corresponding to <math id="Thmtheorem24.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}^{d}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Pic</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}^{d}{\mathcal{C}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, one can use the solution of the Francetta Conjecture for moduli
spaces of curves with a level <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, §12]</cite> to prove that if
<math id="S7.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> the image of the restriction map</p>
<table id="S7.Ex89" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex89.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Z}})\to H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}[l],H_{\mathbb{Z}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g},H_{\mathbb{Z}})\to H^{2}({\Gamma}_{g}[l],H_{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p8.2" class="ltx_p">is <math id="S7.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}/(2g-2){\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}/(2g-2){\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S7.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and <math id="S7.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}/(g-1){\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}/(g-1){\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S7.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> is even and
positive, where <math id="S7.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{g}[l]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{g}[l]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the level <math id="S7.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> subgroup of <math id="S7.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We do not know how to prove surjectivity when <math id="S7.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
